# Checkwright's gate-binary target roster — gate-sdk/SPEC.md §Consumer payload.
# One Rust target triple per line; `#`-comments and blank lines are stripped,
# the same line grammar scripts/gates.list uses. This file is the single surface
# asserting which platforms a released gate binary is built for: the publish
# workflow derives its build matrix from it, and the bootstrap reads the payload
# copy of it to tell "this platform was never committed to" (a refusal that names
# the platform and asks nothing of the adopter) from "committed to, artifact
# missing" (a refusal that names a broken payload). Both refuse; neither proceeds.
# That promise had exactly one violation and it is closed: neither `uname` nor
# .NET's OSArchitecture can tell glibc from musl, so a musl x86_64 host resolved
# to a rostered triple, the roster comparison never fired, and the adopter was
# handed a binary that dies in the dynamic loader. Both bootstraps now answer the
# libc question at the selection step — musl and an unidentifiable libc each
# refuse by name — so the sentence above describes a promise without an exception
# rather than one a reader should assume never had one.
#
# WHAT LICENSES A LINE HERE, stated mechanically so the session that observes
# the green executes rather than re-derives. A target <T> joins when ONE run,
# at or after this paragraph's landing commit, carries BOTH of:
#
#   1. `native-artifacts` green for <T> in .github/workflows/gates.yml — its
#      binary and .sha256 sidecar produced by the release's own build command
#      and uploaded; and
#   2. a platform install-smoke leg green having CONSUMED that upload —
#      INSTALLER_SMOKE_ARTIFACTS_DIR pointed at the downloaded directory — and
#      having reached the artifact-present branch, whose completion assertion
#      installer/consumer-smoke/run-smoke.sh owns and emits.
#
# That is *produced and exercised* with no host-built stand-in anywhere in it.
# On that observation, three edits and nothing else: this file gains <T>'s line,
# native/runners.list already names its runner, and docs/install.md
# §Requirements' platform declaration flips <T> from `held` to `joined`. Those
# three move together or not at all. What that sentence does not say is what
# lands FIRST, and a reader supplying the natural order gets it backwards: the
# `held` DECLARATION is what creates <T>'s producer leg — gates.yml's roster job
# parses docs/install.md and only then looks the runner up, refusing loudly on an
# unmapped target — so the declaration and the runner mapping land in one commit,
# well before the observation, and a runners.list line alone creates nothing.
#
# ONE OBLIGATION THE FIRST OF THOSE THREE INCURS. The binary's source stamp is
# git's content identity for EVERY tracked file under the crate root
# (check-gate-binary-fresh, via git ls-files), and this file is one. So a line
# added here reds that gate with no Rust touched, and the same commit owes
# `bash gate-sdk/bin/build-native.sh`. native/runners.list is in the same set.
#
# **Ruled by the OPERATOR 2026-09-07**, through the lead's question relay in a
# lead session and lead-relayed. The alternative — reading one green macOS
# install-smoke leg as sufficient — was put and refused, on the ground that the
# leg's own header pre-refuses exactly that reading. A later session finding the
# textual case persuasive is reading a settled question.
#
# THE OWNER OF THE WIDENING IS THE PREDICATE ABOVE, not a queue entry. An entry
# is a lifetime that ends; pointing a live precondition at one is what left this
# file naming `platform-support-ci-matrix` for weeks after that entry retired.
#
# Every line here is evidence rather than capability, and the roster's size
# argues nothing either way. The two are easy to conflate here, and conflating
# them is what makes a roster widen on a plan instead of on a run:
#   - A line means a discharged precondition and nothing weaker: the run above
#     is what §Consumer payload's join bound asks for, and a triple without one
#     stays off this file however reachable it looks. x86_64-pc-windows-msvc is
#     the worked case carried all the way through: it compiled for months, which
#     was the capability and never the bound, and it stayed off this file for
#     exactly that long. It joined on 2026-09-09 when one run carried both halves
#     and not on the day it became reachable. Every line here has that shape.
#   - Runner availability is not the constraint. This repository is public, so
#     macOS and arm64-Linux hosted runners are already available to it; what is
#     missing is a run bought on one, not a machine to buy it on. Measured
#     2026-08-25. A header claiming the workflows have no runner for a platform
#     would be reading a fact about this repo as a fact about the world.
#   - Cross-compiling a target from the Linux runner is refused for the reason
#     the bound gives rather than on cost: it would publish an artifact no run
#     has ever executed. `native-artifacts` therefore builds each target on that
#     target's own platform (native/runners.list) and never with `--target`
#     pointed off the host.
#   - WSL is Linux and resolves to this same target, so docs/install.md
#     §Requirements' Windows-through-WSL route is served by the Linux line and
#     keeps being served by it. That route is a CHOICE beside native Windows
#     rather than a substitute for it, now that the native line is here.
#   - The windows line rests on run 34389015132: `native-artifacts` built the
#     binary and its sidecar on `windows-latest`, and the Windows install-smoke
#     leg installed THAT upload — `adopted checkwright-gates.exe ... from the
#     hand-off, sidecar and all — nothing rebuilt, nothing rehashed` — reached
#     the artifact-present branch at every profile and finished
#     `INSTALLER-SMOKE: clean`. Both halves, one run, no host-built stand-in.
#
# This is not a narrowing of the trajectory — the objectives are the direction
# and this roster is the tree. What keeps the UNROSTERED-HOST refusal the normal
# path — rather than the dead code the vacuous-green class exists to refuse —
# is the DECLARED-AND-HELD platform plus the UNDECLARED COMPLEMENT, never the
# roster's size: a native Windows host meets the refusal as a held platform
# whose artifact is not yet published, and every host outside docs/install.md
# §Requirements' declaration block meets it as one the page never declared. A
# target joins only on the observation above, and never beyond what that
# section already states.
#
# The behind-invoke relocation changed what that population MEETS and not how
# large it is: an unrostered host is refused outright rather than served a
# smaller battery, because nothing remains behind the invoke for it to proceed
# into (installer/README.md §The gate binary).
#
# What a further line costs is measured rather than predicted, twice over now:
# one runner mapping in native/runners.list, and nothing else. The consumer smoke
# never blocked on it — it steers itself at its own host and takes a producer's
# artifact directory through INSTALLER_SMOKE_ARTIFACTS_DIR — and the release path
# takes no new surface, its build matrix being roster-derived. The one-time items
# a second line did buy are paid and they generalize: the platform floor and the
# resolved hasher, at scripts/ci-macos-floor.sh and scripts/ci-build-artifact.sh,
# resolve every path they write through `brew --prefix` rather than hard-coding
# one host class's prefix, and so carried a second macOS class with no edit. A
# further line on a platform class those two already bootstrap therefore costs
# nothing on that path, and one on a class they do not costs its own floor first.
# gate-sdk/SPEC.md §Consumer payload owns both halves;
# installer/README.md §The consumer smoke owns the smoke's own side of it.
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
aarch64-apple-darwin
x86_64-apple-darwin
x86_64-pc-windows-msvc
